I've had plenty of games where it's been a case of:

Head across large open area, because I've finished exploring this batch
of twisty passages without finding a means to progress, so I've *got* to
cross this open area.

Halfway across large open area, hear shout.

.oO(Crap. That's a centaur standing in that side passage that's well
within detection radius. Panic! He's faster than me and nastier than me.)

"The bolt of flame hits you!"

.oO(Ow.)

The thing with Crawl is that although alternative approaches to ID are
extremely limited (shops are rare, and you can only identify bad things
by buying them, not by selling them, because you can't sell things),
potions (degeneration, slowing, poison, strong poison), scrolls
(immolation, torment), and wands whose use (hasting, healing, ...) by
you can lead directly to your death still exist.

You can't even have any faith in statistically-guided
identification-by-use; I've found my stack of five identical potions to
be slowing or confusion or poison or degeneration far too many times,
and because you can't see what kind of potion/scroll/wand the monsters
are using (only that they're using *something* of that type), you can't
even get the monsters to identify things for you.
